WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.150
For many analysts, Linux is a blind spot — all the detection attention goes to Windows and Active Directory.

2
00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:14.470
But the web servers, databases, and internet-facing boxes that run the most exposed workloads? Those are Linux.

3
00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:21.260
Attackers know this. A public-facing Linux web server is often the very first thing they touch.

4
00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:27.500
If your team can't read a Linux log, the most exposed part of your estate is also the least defended.

5
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.380
Four words first. A shell is the program that reads what you type and runs it — usually bash.

6
00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:39.520
A process is a running program, with a PID and a parent — just like Windows.

7
00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:45.540
Users and permissions decide who can read, write, and execute — and root can do anything.

8
00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:52.600
And a daemon is a background service — sshd, cron, nginx — the Linux equivalent of a Windows service.

9
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:59.340
Now the logs. auth.log is your first stop — logons, sudo, and SSH activity.

10
00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:03.440
syslog, or journald, carries system and service messages.

11
00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:08.340
Web server access and error logs are the front door on an internet-facing box.

12
00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:13.330
auditd gives detailed execution and file-access auditing when you've enabled it.

13
00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:18.920
And bash history shows the actual commands a user ran — gold during an investigation.

14
00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:23.790
One pattern every analyst must recognize: the reverse shell.

15
00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:38.960
A command like bash -i redirecting to /dev/tcp of some external IP and port means the server just dialed out to an attacker and handed them a live shell. In a process list or bash history, that line is the whole incident.

16
00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:42.510
So give Linux the same attention as Windows.

17
00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:49.830
Read auth.log for access, check cron for persistence, and scan the process list for shells that shouldn't exist.
